    GPU Metal: optionally limit the GPU work in flight
    
    At 20 kHz occupancy the macOS GPU watchdog killed command buffers, and
    reset the desktop once, while all 8 streams ran multi-kernel buffers of
    up to ~1 s at the same time. Host-serialised execution (serializeGPU 3)
    was the only mode that survived. Add two runtime limits, off by default:
    
    - GPUCA_METAL_MAX_KERNELS_PER_BUFFER=N commits a stream's command buffer
      after N kernels, so that long chains (sector tracking, SectorRefit,
      TrackFit) become short buffers. Buffers of a stream stay ordered by its
      order event, the events of the other streams are untouched.
    - GPUCA_METAL_MAX_STREAMS_IN_FLIGHT=M makes the host wait, before it
      commits a buffer on an idle stream, until fewer than M streams have
      committed buffers that are not complete. This cannot deadlock: a buffer
      only waits for events signalled by buffers committed before it, since
      RecordMarker commits the signal at once, so the work in flight completes
      without the host.
    
    pbpb30, M1 Max, 6 rounds of 10 events (timeline: longest buffer, streams
    running at once): default unchanged (931 ms/ev, -3 ms over two run orders);
    N=1 M=1 1416 ms/ev, 87 ms, 1 stream (serializeGPU 3: 1413 ms/ev);
    N=1 M=2 1100 ms/ev, 87 ms, 2; N=2 M=4 941 ms/ev, 123 ms, 4. The longest
    buffers left hold the TrackFit kernel (~85 ms alone). Metal dumps byte-identical on
    pbpb30 with and without the limits, sim5pp identical to the CPU.

