    GPU Metal: make command-buffer failures fatal
    
    A failed Metal command buffer (e.g. killed by the macOS GPU watchdog,
    kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorImpactingInteractivity / ...Timeout) was
    only logged when the stream was drained, and the reconstruction carried on
    without its work: on 20 kHz Pb-Pb time frames this gave silently wrong
    outputs (271,967-296,957 tracks vs 297,669 on CPU for the same TF).
    Worse, a failed buffer never signals the events it was to signal, so
    later buffers waiting for them on the GPU, and the host waiting for those,
    blocked forever: one such run hung at 0% CPU.
    
    Signal the stream-order event and the recorded event of a buffer from its
    completion handler when it fails, so everything queued behind it
    completes, and fail in DrainStream through GPUChkErr, like the CUDA and
    HIP backends do on a failed synchronisation ("GPU Backend Failure").
    Failures seen while the device is torn down are only logged, so the
    destructor does not throw.
    
    pbpb30 (M1 Max, abp.sh, 3 rounds, --runs 10): 966.7 ms/ev before, 962.5
    after; Metal dumps byte-identical before and after; sim5pp dump still
    identical to CPU.

