    GPU: wait for the O2 track count before sorting the O2 output tracks
    
    With GPUCA_SPECIALIZE_THRUST_SORTS (CUDA and HIP), the host-side launch
    of GPUTPCGMO2Output::sort takes the number of elements from the host
    copy of the merger's memory, processors()->tpcMerger.NOutputTracksTPCO2().
    That count is computed by GPUTPCGMO2Output::prepare on the device, and
    TransferMemoryResourceLinkToHost brings it to the host asynchronously,
    on the same stream, right before the sort is launched. The event of the
    transfer was only waited for after the sort, so the sort very likely
    got the value from before prepare ran: 0, since RunTPCTrackingMerger
    clears the merger's memory on the host at its start, and the transfer
    is queued behind prepare. Then the O2 output tracks keep the order in
    which prepare wrote them. This goes back to b0155c276877, which moved
    the wait after the sort. With debugLevel >= 1 or serializeGPU the
    transfer is synchronous, which would hide it.
    
    The other sorts that read host counts, SortTracks and SortTracksQPt,
    wait for their transfer with CondWaitEvent first; do the same here, by
    waiting before the sort. The wait was already needed before the
    allocation of the O2 output that follows, so it costs no extra
    synchronisation.
    
    Found by reading the code, while porting these sorts to the Metal
    backend, which reads the count after draining the stream. Not tested on
    CUDA or HIP, there is no NVIDIA or AMD GPU here; the CPU build compiles.
    To check on a GPU: with debugLevel 0, outputTracksTPCO2 should come out
    ordered by the sort's comparator with this change, and not without it.

