    DPL: rely only on uv_timer to decide when to create timer inputs
    
    This gets rid of the check of how much time has elapsed since the previous timer input slot creation.
    Instead, we check if the associated uv_timer has indeed fired before the current loop iteration.
    Checking if the uv_timer fired using `uv_timer_get_due_in` did not work, since it does not return 0
    for repeating timers, while comparing the return value to the output of `uv_timer_get_repeat` may
    return false results if our loop is called e.g. twice in the same millisecond.
    
    It could be improved by calling uv_timer_start only once after we move to the steady period.

