Author: Sandro Wenzel <sandro.wenzel@cern.ch>
Date:   Tue Jun 5 17:03:14 2018 +0200

    Introducing collaborative simulation using FairMQ devices
    
    This is a commit providing (the first version of) a major development
    It introduces parallel simulation capabilities, in which
    multiple simulation workers collaborate on the simulation of
    a set of events. The system is implemented using FairMQ devices
    communicating via messages.
    
    The key components of the system are
    
    1) O2PrimaryServerDevice
    ------------------------
    
    A server device, generating events (primaries) using some
    event generator and serving chunks of primaries to
    simulation workers.
    
    2) O2SimDevice:
    ---------------
    
    A worker device actually performing a VMC simulation on a chunk
    of primaries. The chunk can be smaller than a full event so that
    multiple workers can collaboratively simulate a big event.
    Simulation products (hits) are forwarded to a merger device which
    is responsible to do the IO
    
    3) O2HitMergerDevice:
    ---------------------
    
    The device aggregating hits from the workers and doing IO to
    a ROOT file.
    
    Other notes:
    
    - A driver application `o2sim_parallel` is provided that
      sets up the system behind the scenes and which feels like
      the standard o2sim executable but parallelized:
    
        o2sim_parallel -m PIPE ITS TPC -g pythia8 -e TGeant4 -n 20
    
    - A couple of important changes accompagny this commit
    
      *) introduction of an O2MCApplication customizing certain aspects
         of FairMCApplication
    
      *) addition of O2RunSim which sets up O2MCApplication
    
      *) addition of some members to the base Detector class, amending
         it to prepare hit messages for a given FairMQChannel
    
      *) modification of the o2sim.C macro in order to be able
         to compile for the old o2sim executable as well as the new
         parallel system
    
    - The fact that we process sub-events (aka primary chunks) might
      make the handling of MCTruth etc more complex in the future

