    Second iteration on the DataProcessingLayer
    
    * Add parallel / merge constructs to workflow
    
    This is a first attempt at allowing expressing parallel processing and merging
    of multiple streams of data in the declarative workflow.
    
    It introduces two helper functions:
    
        o2::framework::parallel(...)
    
    which allows duplicating and amending a template `DataProcessorSpec`, and
    
        o2::framework::mergeInputs(..)
    
    which allows for duplicating InputSpec of a given device. Context information
    for parallel computations (e.g. rank and number of parallel devices) can be
    retrieved in the `ProcessCallback` via the `ParallelContext` service.
    
    * Add time pipelining to the workflow
    
    It's now possible to specify that a given `DataProcessor` will actually
    be split in N devices, where each of the N devices takes one of the
    timeslices produced by the previous step.
    
    * Inputs now wrapped in InputRecord
    
    Rather than exposing raw vector of DataRef to the algorithm writers, we
    provide an InputRecord class which offers helper methods to access the
    received inputs.
    
    * ProcessingContext, InitContext, ErrorContext introduced
    
    In order to simplify writing Algorithm callbacks, the AlgorithmSpec API has
    been changed to take a "catch all" `*Context` object which exposes whatever is
    relevant for the computation. This means that an algorithm spec will go from:
    
        AlgorithmSpec{
          [](InputRecord &, ServiceRegistry &, DataAllocator &){
          }
        }
    
    to
    
        AlgorithmSpec{
          [](ProcessingContext &) {
          }
        }
    
    * Improved internals
    
    A lot of improvements to the internals have been done. In particular:
    
      - There is now one channel per device - device connection rather than one
        channel per exchanged datatype.
      - DataRelayer now handles more that 64 kind of inputs per device.
      - Increased number of tests and code coverage deriving from them.

