    Using smart pointers to correctly cleanup nodes from Huffman tree
    
    Fixes a memory leak caused by the allocated new nodes in the binary
    tree sorting. Algorithm is based on combining pointers to nodes in
    a new node which had so far been allocated using new operator. The
    cleanup has been ignored so far, and needs a bit bookkeeping since
    the original leave nodes are owned by the vector of leave nodes,
    while all tree nodes are owned by their parent nodes. Introducing
    shared_ptr solves this problem.
    
    TODO: Performance still needs to be checked, maybe some counts of
    the shared pointer can be avoided using references.
    
    Further changes:
    - using compiler default for copy ctor and assignment operator.
    - checking for empty node list at write out

